					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wishun" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1204404); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1204404&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608930948" alt="wishun" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1204404)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wishun" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1204404); return false;" >wishun</a></span> <span class="odate time_1356634920 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">27 Dec 2012 19:02</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1671686">
									

<p>I see, I see&#8230;<br />
I'm not used to thinking of AWCY? in that way, I generally consider them to be your standard art-school douches with unexplained access to anomalous tech. Usually nonsensical and frequently caked with irony, but ultimately earnest in their art/philosophy BS. But this idea of AWCY? as an inside joke that got taken literally and now has a life of its own, that is rather compelling. If you aproach the group from that perspective, your message in the tale is clear and well-executed. It's not a failing on your part if some of us readers have a different point of view that twists things up. Gotta love the non-canon!</p>
<p>Now back to what I was saying origianlly. If I've read your post correctly, and stop me if I've made some false conclusions, this is concretly not &quot;an AWCY? tale&quot; because it predates their founding. I can see the logic. But, to my mind that isn't sufficient for exclusion. It is a story about anomalous, guerilla art, with a stated goal of recontextualizing the nature of the art/reality/artist relationships at all costs. It is a story about an artist who believes that an artistic statement is vastly more important than human life and suffering. Indeed, an artist who manifestly believes that pain and death are a valid choice of medium. To my mind, this is a story about an AWCY? performance that just happened to be born before its time.</p>
<p>Think of it this way. If it was discovered that an 18th century painter had created a number of portaits where the subject was depicted in a stylized, seemingly disassembled form, with the apparant intent of portraying several viewpoints at once; that painter would be deemed a &quot;cubist&quot;. Regardless of the fact that he died 100 years before Picasso started the movement. If it looks like a duck and quacks like a duck, it's probably Michael Winslow in a Daffy suit.</p>

							</div>

								
					
